  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "age-05-multi-person-age-systems",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Three or More People and Linked Age Ratios",
    "description": "Connect several people’s ages through ratios, totals, and shared relationships without losing track of each person.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use short, connected phone-sized teaching screens with concrete ages, labelled dates, separate calculation lines, a reason for each step, and an original-clue check. Place a fresh matching practice question after each worked example; wrong answers receive a worked correction and parallel practice.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 8, Problems on Ages. Approved scope: Solve age systems involving three or more people, such as linked pairwise ratios, combined age totals, and parent/child/grandparent relationships. Assign a clear variable to a common scale or one person's age; translate each ratio at its correct time; and connect the equations without treating pairwise ratio units as interchangeable. Teach a table of person, present age, and shifted age to prevent accidental role swaps. Include a system where a sum and a linked ratio determine the scale, and one where a redundant relationship serves as a check. Learners should count people affected by a total's time shift, solve the linked equations, test each stated condition, and report the requested age rather than an intermediate variable."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's connect age clues about three people.\n\nIf A:B and B:C are given, B appears in both clues. We can use that shared person to place all three ages on one scale.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose A:B = 3:4 and B:C = 2:3.\n\nB is four parts in the first ratio but two parts in the second. We cannot join the two lists until B has the same number of parts.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Double every part of B:C:\nB:C = 4:6.\n\nNow the shared B is four parts in both ratios, so A:B:C = 3:4:6 and A:C = 3:6 = 1:2. Changing both numbers in a ratio by the same factor keeps the relationship.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
