    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A known age can also set the scale. Suppose P:Q = 4:5, Q:R = 2:3, and Q is 30.\n\nUse the first ratio: five Q-parts equal 30, so one part equals 6.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3_step": {
      "id": "teach_3_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "P = 4 × 6 = 24.\n\nFor Q:R = 2:3, two Q-parts equal 30, so one part equals 15 and R = 45.\n\nCheck P:Q = 24:30 = 4:5 and Q:R = 30:45 = 2:3. The two ratios can use different part sizes, but Q must be the same real age.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_3"
        }
      ]
    },
